<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What the modules' infrastructure projects do the same way, and none of them owns: today one check, that the
    marker a page is asked with is a cursor of the list being paged. Every module pages its lists by GreenDonut's
    paging over Entity Framework, each in its own infrastructure project, and a module does not reference
    another's; so what all of them need of that paging lives here, once.

    No [assembly: Module] in this project, on purpose: it belongs to no module, so every module may name what it
    declares. It references no module and knows nothing of any of them: a list hands it its query and the refusal
    to answer with. Only a module's infrastructure project references it, since it reads how a query is ordered
    and how the paging library writes a cursor, which no other layer knows. Keep it small: a type belongs here only
    when every module's storage would otherwise carry a copy of it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- PagingArguments, and the paging library's own reading of a cursor. The paging library alone: no GraphQL comes with it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="GreenDonut.Data" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
